  /// every boot. The streamed path keeps memory flat and UTF-8-decodes
  /// only lines that pass the byte-level gate (type names are ASCII, so a
  /// byte scan is exact); line boundaries are found on raw bytes, which
  /// is UTF-8-safe (`0x0A` never appears inside a multi-byte sequence).
  ///
  /// With [lastSegmentOnly] only the PRIMARY segment (the live file) is
  /// scanned instead of the whole `.part-NN` chain — O(tail) cost for
  /// boot-path callers whose records are tail-appended during runs
  /// (gh-1449's orphan-report seed). The trade-off is explicit: records
  /// already rotated into an older segment are invisible to the scan, so
  /// such a caller may re-report once after a resume; use it only where
  /// that degradation is acceptable and the chain walk must not sit on
  /// the boot path (the #503 precedent).
  ///
  /// gh-1734 scan memo: the result is memoized per (session path, scan
  /// parameters, chain fingerprint) — the boot/switch path re-scans the
  /// same chain on every open/switch (obligations ledger, orphan
  /// reports, subagent registry, steering), a 95MB-tail read each time
  /// on a marathon file. An unchanged chain (same per-file size+mtime)
  /// is served from the memo without touching the file; any
  /// append/rotation/truncate changes a stat and re-scans. A memo hit
  /// returns a fresh list of SHARED record instances — the codebase
  /// treats custom records as immutable (read-only payload consumers);
  /// mutating them would corrupt future hits.
  Future<List<CustomRecord>> readCustomRecordsOfType(
    SessionMetadata metadata,
    Set<String> types, {
    bool lastSegmentOnly = false,
    // Non-null default so an EXPLICIT `null` is distinguishable from
    // omitting the parameter (review: `?? cap` here made the documented
    // unbounded opt-out a silent no-op — null coalesced to the default).
    int? tailCapBytes = kSessionSegmentHardCapBytes,
  }) async {
    // Rotated sessions (gh-1077) keep older records in `.part-NN`
    // segments: scan the whole chain in order or registry snapshots
    // flushed before the last rotation become invisible to subagent
    // adoption (#488), memory refresh and ttsr. Dedupe by record id:
    // between a failed rotation seed and the next open the archived
    // part and the restored primary hold the SAME records — the open
    // path dedupes (seenRecordIds), the raw scan must too (round 5).
    //
    // Tail cap (the goal_builder 2.7GB incident): the windowed storage
    // never rotates, so a marathon session's PRIMARY grows unbounded
    // (measured 2.7GB against the 80MB rotate / 95MB hard-cap design),
    // and a from-offset-0 scan of it sat on the resume boot path for
    // 10+ seconds (orphan-report seed) and churned post-boot
    // (steering, obligations). Every consumer today is a TAIL consumer
    // (`records.last`, or pending-vs-consumed steering where a pair
    // entirely beyond the cap cancels itself), so the default bounds
    // each FILE to the tail region a properly rotated primary promised
    // ([kSessionSegmentHardCapBytes]); rotated `.part-NN` files are
    // smaller than that and scan in full. Deep-history callers pass an
    // EXPLICIT `tailCapBytes: null` (the parameter defaults to the cap,
    // so only an explicit null is unbounded).
    final cap = tailCapBytes;
    final paths = await listSessionSegmentPaths(_fs, metadata.path);
    final scanned = lastSegmentOnly ? paths.sublist(paths.length - 1) : paths;
    // gh-1734 scan memo: the boot/switch path re-scans the same chain on
    // EVERY open/switch (obligations ledger, orphan reports, subagent
    // registry, steering) — a 95MB-tail read each time on a marathon
    // file. The scan is a pure function of the scanned bytes, so a
    // (path, params, size, mtime)-keyed memo serves an unchanged chain
    // without touching it; any append/rotation/truncate changes a stat
    // and misses. Records are SHARED between callers — the codebase
    // treats them as immutable (read-only payload consumers).
